        <section class="my-3" aria-labelledby="mnemonic-faq">
    <h2 id="mnemonic-faq">Mnemonic Converter FAQ</h2>

    <div class="accordion" id="mnemonicFaqAccordion">
    <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordion9d57d8c22d" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordion9d57d8c22d">
            What is an HD wallet?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordion9d57d8c22d" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>An <strong>HD wallet</strong>, or hierarchical deterministic wallet, derives a tree of private keys,
                public keys, and addresses from a single master seed. Because the keys are generated deterministically,
                the same seed, optional passphrase, and derivation paths recreate the same wallet.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">HD wallets make it possible to back up many accounts and addresses with one seed phrase.
                They also support extended keys: an extended public key can generate and monitor addresses without
                spending funds, while an extended private key can derive the private keys for its branch and must be
                protected like the seed phrase.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordione40493faa8" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordione40493faa8">
            What is a mnemonic seed phrase?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordione40493faa8" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>A <strong>mnemonic phrase</strong>, also called a seed phrase or recovery phrase, is an ordered list of
                words used to back up a cryptocurrency wallet. A <a
                    href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki">BIP-39 mnemonic</a> usually
                contains 12 or 24 words, but 15, 18, and 21-word phrases are also valid.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">Strictly speaking, the mnemonic is the human-readable backup. Wallet software combines it
                with an optional passphrase to create a binary <strong>seed</strong>, then derives the wallet's master
                key, private keys, and addresses from that seed.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordion7debc9dcac" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordion7debc9dcac">
            Which mnemonic and seed types does this converter support?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordion7debc9dcac" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>This converter supports four input modes:</p>

            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li><strong>BIP-39:</strong> Standard mnemonic phrases with an optional BIP-39 passphrase.</li>
                <li><strong>BIP-39 (Electrum):</strong> <a
                        href="https://electrum.readthedocs.io/en/latest/seedphrase.html">Electrum seed phrases</a> with
                    automatic or custom derivation paths.</li>
                <li><strong>BIP-32:</strong> A raw hexadecimal seed used to derive a <a
                        href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0032.mediawiki">hierarchical deterministic
                        wallet</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>Electrum (legacy):</strong> Legacy Electrum mnemonic phrases and their deterministic key
                    sequence.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordion2a890e9f14" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordion2a890e9f14">
            What is a derivation path?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordion2a890e9f14" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>A <strong>derivation path</strong> tells a hierarchical deterministic wallet which branch of its key
                tree to use. A path such as <code>m/84'/0'/0'/0</code> identifies the purpose, coin type, account, and
                change branch. The converter appends an address index to the selected base path.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">Common standards include <a
                    href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0044.mediawiki">BIP-44</a> for legacy
                addresses, <a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0049.mediawiki">BIP-49</a> for
                wrapped SegWit, <a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0084.mediawiki">BIP-84</a> for
                native SegWit, and <a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0086.mediawiki">BIP-86</a>
                for Taproot. An apostrophe after a path level, such as <code>84'</code>, marks that level as hardened.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordionf9ec11c9f4" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordionf9ec11c9f4">
            What is hardened derivation?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordionf9ec11c9f4" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p><strong>Hardened derivation</strong> is a BIP-32 method for deriving a child key that requires the parent
                private key. Unlike a normal, non-hardened child, a hardened child cannot be derived from the parent
                extended public key. Hardened levels are written with an apostrophe or <code>h</code>, for example
                <code>m/44'/0'/0'</code> or <code>m/44h/0h/0h</code>.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">Hardened derivation isolates branches of an HD wallet and prevents a parent extended
                private key from being reconstructed from its extended public key and an exposed child private key.
                The tradeoff is that a watch-only wallet cannot derive addresses below a hardened level unless it is
                given an extended public key from a point after that level.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mnemonicFaqAccordion67d582c923" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="mnemonicFaqAccordion67d582c923">
            Why can the same mnemonic phrase generate different addresses?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="mnemonicFaqAccordion67d582c923" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#mnemonicFaqAccordion">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p class="mb-0">A mnemonic does not map to only one address. The selected cryptocurrency network,
                mnemonic type, optional passphrase, derivation path, address index, and address format all affect the
                result. To restore the same wallet, use the same phrase, passphrase, derivation standard, and network
                settings that the original wallet used.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
